.\" $OpenBSD$
.\" Public domain.
.Dd $Mdocdate: September 6 2026 $
.Dt BTRFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm btrfs
.Nd inspect and administer Btrfs filesystems
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm device add
.Op Fl f
.Ar device ...
.Ar mountpoint
.Nm
.Cm device remove
.Ar device-or-id ...
.Ar mountpoint
.Nm
.Cm subvolume list
.Ar mountpoint
.Nm
.Cm subvolume create
.Ar mountpoint path
.Nm
.Cm subvolume delete
.Ar mountpoint path
.Nm
.Cm subvolume snapshot
.Op Fl r
.Ar mountpoint source path
.Nm
.Cm send
.Op Fl f Ar stream
.Op Fl p Ar parent
.Ar mountpoint path
.Nm
.Cm receive
.Op Fl f Ar stream
.Ar mountpoint path
.Nm
.Cm inspect-internal
.Ar command ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility inspects Btrfs filesystems and administers their devices and subvolumes.
Use
.Fl Fl help
after a command to display its usage.
.Pp
Device commands select a writable filesystem by
.Ar mountpoint
and require superuser privileges.
Device paths name block devices, such as
.Pa /dev/sd2a ,
rather than raw character devices.
Multiple devices are processed in order, stopping at the first failure.
.Bl -tag -width "device remove"
.It Cm device add
Add devices to a mounted SINGLE/DUP filesystem.
Devices may have different sizes.
Each device contributes its partition size, rounded down to a filesystem
sector boundary.
Existing chunks retain their profiles and placement; subsequent chunk growth
can use the added space.
Both copies of a DUP chunk reside on one device.
The
.Xr df 1
display reports allocated chunk capacity; it grows as chunks are allocated
from the device pool.
.Pp
By default, nonzero bytes preceding the first superblock or in any superblock
location prevent addition.
The
.Fl f
option permits overwriting those signatures.
Mounted or busy devices are rejected even with
.Fl f .
.It Cm device remove
Move allocated chunks onto remaining devices, then detach the selected member.
Specify a block-device path or a numeric device ID.
The last device cannot be removed.
Removal preserves logical extent addresses, snapshots, reflinks, compression,
and checksums.
Live extents are copied and checksummed copies are validated before each new
chunk mapping is committed.
System chunks, device accounting, and bootstrap mappings are updated as part
of the same commit.
Successful detachment clears the removed device's superblocks.
.El
.Pp
For subvolume, send, and receive commands,
.Ar mountpoint
selects a mounted Btrfs filesystem.
All source and destination paths start at the top-level subvolume (ID 5),
regardless of which subvolume is mounted there.
A leading slash is optional.
Parent components
.Pq Sq ..
and symbolic links are not followed.
The destination parent directory must already exist.
These commands require superuser privileges and use
.Pa /dev/btrfs-control .
.Bl -tag -width "subvolume snapshot"
.It Cm subvolume list
List all linked subvolumes in the filesystem, including their IDs,
parent subvolume IDs, read-only state, and filesystem-relative paths.
The top-level subvolume is implicit and is not listed.
.It Cm subvolume create
Create an empty writable subvolume.
.It Cm subvolume delete
Remove the named subvolume and its contents.
A subvolume with nested subvolumes must have those removed first.
Mounted subvolumes and subvolumes with active vnodes cannot be deleted.
.It Cm subvolume snapshot
Create a writable snapshot of the exact source subvolume.
The
.Fl r
option creates a read-only snapshot.
Snapshots share metadata and file-data extents with their source;
later modifications use copy-on-write.
.It Cm send
Write a Linux version 1 send stream for the named read-only subvolume.
With
.Fl p ,
send changes relative to the named read-only parent.
The receiver must already have a matching parent, identified by UUID and
sender transaction ID.
Parent and clone-source lookup rejects ambiguous read-only received identities.
These lookups use the filesystem's UUID index when current, falling back to
a scan of subvolume root items when the index is absent or stale.
Without
.Fl f ,
write to standard output; terminal output is rejected.
With
.Fl f ,
create a new stream file at the given host pathname.
.It Cm receive
Replay Linux version 1 streams below the given filesystem-relative directory.
Read standard input or the host file named by
.Fl f .
Each stream names the new subvolume; an existing destination is never replaced.
Concatenated complete streams are accepted.
Successful completion records the received identity and makes the new tree
read-only in one transaction.
An error leaves an incomplete writable tree without a received identity.
.El
.Pp
For example, if subvolume
.Pa home
is mounted at
.Pa /home ,
the following creates its sibling snapshot without mounting ID 5:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
btrfs subvolume snapshot -r /home home home@monday
btrfs send -f monday.stream /home home@monday
btrfs receive -f monday.stream /backup snapshots
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/btrfs-control"
.It Pa /dev/btrfs-control
Filesystem administration device.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mount_btrfs 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Device commands are synchronous.
Filesystem writers wait during device administration; reads may continue.
Removal requires enough unallocated physical space on a remaining member
for each whole chunk, including both DUP copies.
It does not compact partially used chunks or change profiles.
Device resizing and balance are not implemented.
.Pp
A failed removal can leave a completed prefix of chunk moves.
The member stays attached until the final membership commit succeeds.
An I/O or metadata error can make all views read-only.
If clearing the removed device fails after detachment, the command reports an
error but the device is already detached.
After an interrupted addition, supply both the old members and the new member
when recovering a published addition.
After detachment, supply only the remaining members.
Update explicit device lists in mount commands or
.Xr fstab 5
to reflect membership changes.
.Pp
Subvolume deletion currently reserves metadata for the complete operation.
It may fail for lack of metadata space even when deleting the subvolume
would eventually free enough space.
Listing is ordered by subvolume ID; concurrent commands can change the
list between calls.
.Pp
Send and receive reuse a reachable mount view or mount a disjoint subvolume
temporarily under
.Pa /tmp .
The receive destination must remain private during replay.
Finalization rejects active vnodes and mounted descendant views.
Normal errors and catchable termination signals remove temporary mounts;
an uncatchable termination may require manual unmounting.
.Pp
The sender inventories names in userspace and compares read-only snapshot
trees through the kernel, skipping shared metadata subtrees.
File extent records identify unchanged data and parent clone ranges.
Only data needed for WRITE commands is read; sparse files are traversed by
their extent records rather than their logical size.
Pathname inventories still require memory proportional to the namespace.
Clone sources are limited to matching inode identities and file offsets in
the parent; equal contents in unrelated allocations are sent again.
CLONE commands share source data extents, including compressed extent slices.
Inline source data is copied because it has no separate data allocation.
Source and destination offsets must be sector aligned; a partial final sector
must end at source EOF and at or beyond destination EOF.
Sparse contents are preserved, but some holes may become allocated zero data.
Linux extended attributes are opaque data on OpenBSD; Linux ACLs and security
labels do not establish OpenBSD access-control policy.
As with Linux receive, ctime is local and symlink permissions are not transmitted.
Version 2/3 streams, no-data streams, nested subvolumes, and inode flags
are not supported.
